    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1308">

                <pb facs="1038" n="922" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg922"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.77/132.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President to the Acting Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cumberland, Md.</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-05-12">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 12, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Have information which would be glad to have you confirm that there are as many
                    as two hundred and fifty American railway conductors and many locomotive
                    engineers and trainmen who have been thrown out of employment in Mexico and are
                    now refugees in El Paso and other border towns. If you can, verify this
                    information by telegraph to consuls at various places and to the Ambassador. I
                    should be glad if you would issue a statement by my direction inviting the
                    attention of railway presidents and managers to the presence <pb facs="1039"
                        n="923" xml:id="pg923"/> of these skilled workmen with a view if possible to
                    securing employment for them and to save them from suffering.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wm. H. Taft.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
